    "comment_count": 1,
    "comment_data": [
        {
            "author": {
                "avatarUrl": "https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/24854248?u=d09fd974c6720eba3039db6beb2f1c1efecdb51d&v=4",
                "login": "echasnovski",
                "resourcePath": "/echasnovski",
                "url": "https://github.com/echasnovski"
            },
            "authorAssociation": "MEMBER",
            "body": "Sorry, I don't have much knowledge of 'lazy.nvim' management.\r\n\r\nMy feeling here is that the whole 'echasnovski/mini.nvim' is lazy loaded in which case there might be issues with 'mini.icons'. The whole 217 line is a LazyVim's way to make 'mini.icons' properly lazy-loadable in a 'lazy.nvim' sense.\r\n\r\nThe suggestion would be to find a way to execute `require('mini.icons').setup()` specifically during startup without lazy loading it. Or use ['mini.deps'](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/readmes/mini-deps.md) :)",
            "bodyHTML": "<p dir=\"auto\">Sorry, I don't have much knowledge of 'lazy.nvim' management.</p>\n<p dir=\"auto\">My feeling here is that the whole 'echasnovski/mini.nvim' is lazy loaded in which case there might be issues with 'mini.icons'. The whole 217 line is a LazyVim's way to make 'mini.icons' properly lazy-loadable in a 'lazy.nvim' sense.</p>\n<p dir=\"auto\">The suggestion would be to find a way to execute <code class=\"notranslate\">require('mini.icons').setup()</code> specifically during startup without lazy loading it. Or use <a href=\"https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/readmes/mini-deps.md\">'mini.deps'</a> :)</p>",
            "bodyText": "Sorry, I don't have much knowledge of 'lazy.nvim' management.\nMy feeling here is that the whole 'echasnovski/mini.nvim' is lazy loaded in which case there might be issues with 'mini.icons'. The whole 217 line is a LazyVim's way to make 'mini.icons' properly lazy-loadable in a 'lazy.nvim' sense.\nThe suggestion would be to find a way to execute require('mini.icons').setup() specifically during startup without lazy loading it. Or use 'mini.deps' :)",
